  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "It used to be the case that if a user provided a filename in a Content-Disposition header, fedora would populate a ebucore:filename property.  Responses would have a Content-Disposition containing a matching filename",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "url": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/browse/FCREPO-2584#icft=FCREPO-2584"
       },
       "type": "inlineCard"
      },
      {
       "text": " eliminated handling of Content-Disposition in requests",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "https://jira.duraspace.org/browse/FCREPO-2584"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "https://jira.duraspace.org/browse/FCREPO-2584",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "As a result, ebucore:filename is always \"\".",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The resolution to this ticket should remove the default population of ebucore:filename, and consider whether or not Content-Disposition should be returned in responses at all.  If it is, then should it have a filename value?  Is this still determined by ebucore:filename (if a user explicitly adds such a triple?)  ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
